# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sonder - environment variables
# Copy to .env (repo root) and fill in. NEVER commit real secrets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Database (SQLite) -----------------------------------------------------
# A `file:` path, created and migrated automatically on first run. The value
# below is for local development.
#
# A RELATIVE path is resolved by Prisma against apps/server/prisma/, which on a
# host means inside the container. Nothing complains: preflight reports the
# database up to date, /health/ready answers database:up, the healthcheck
# passes -- and every deploy and every restart silently erases all accounts,
# messages and call history.
#
# On a host, mount a volume and give this an ABSOLUTE path on it:
#   DATABASE_URL="file:/data/sonder.db"
# scripts/serve.mjs says so at boot if it finds a relative path behind a
# platform edge, because nothing else in the stack ever will.
DATABASE_URL=file:./dev.db

# --- Demo data -------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset. `npm run dev` seeds twelve demo accounts into an empty database, which
# is what makes the local app look like a working social product, and the login
# page publishes their shared password on purpose.
#
# Production start (scripts/serve.mjs) does NOT seed, because creating accounts
# with a published password in somebody's deployment should be a decision, not
# a default. Set this to true if you want the populated demo on a host, knowing
# anyone can sign into those twelve accounts.
# SEED_DEMO_DATA=true

# --- Server ----------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTHING IS SET IN THIS SECTION, ON PURPOSE.
#
# Everything below is determined by *where the process runs*, not by the app, and
# every value already has the right default in apps/server/src/env.ts. Listing
# literals here was actively harmful: this file is what gets pasted into a host's
# variables panel, and scripts/preflight.mjs also generates .env from it on the
# host, so each literal became a production setting nobody chose. Three separate
# deploy failures came from exactly that:
#
#   NODE_ENV=development  -> `next build` aborted with "<Html> should not be
#                            imported outside of pages/_document", which points at
#                            the Pages Router in an App Router app.
#   PORT=4000             -> the single-port proxy bound 4000 and then handed the
#                            API child the same port: EADDRINUSE, restart loop.
#   COOKIE_SECURE=false   -> refresh cookies served without Secure behind HTTPS.
#
# The defaults, for reference:
#   NODE_ENV      development (server). next dev/build/start force their own.
#   PORT          4000        On a host, the platform sets this and serve.mjs
#                             picks non-colliding loopback ports for the children.
#   HOST          0.0.0.0
#   TRUST_PROXY_HOPS  1. How many proxies are in front of the API, which is a
#                 count and not a switch: one too few makes req.ip the nearest
#                 proxy, so every visitor shares one rate-limit bucket and
#                 AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX becomes a site-wide limit. Single-port
#                 hosting has two (platform edge, then serve.mjs) and
#                 scripts/serve.mjs sets it to 2 itself. Do not set it by hand.
#   CORS_ORIGINS  http://localhost:3000, plus any localhost port in development.
#                 In production scripts/serve.mjs adds the platform's public
#                 origin (RAILWAY_PUBLIC_DOMAIN / PUBLIC_URL) automatically.
#
# Set one here only to override it locally, and prefer setting it on the host
# rather than in this file.

# --- Auth ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Left EMPTY on purpose. `npm run dev` (via scripts/ensure-env.mjs) generates a
# random 64-char secret into .env for you. A placeholder here would be long
# enough to pass validation, so copying this file by hand would quietly produce
# an app whose session tokens are signed with a secret published in the repo.
# To set one yourself:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_ACCESS_TTL=15m
JWT_REFRESH_TTL_DAYS=30
COOKIE_DOMAIN=
# COOKIE_SECURE is deliberately unset — see the Server note above. It defaults to
# false (correct for http://localhost), and scripts/serve.mjs sets it to true by
# itself once it detects a TLS-terminating edge in front of it. Setting it false
# here is what caused cookies to be served without Secure over HTTPS.

# --- Rate limiting ---------------------------------------------------------
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=300
AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=20

# --- Frontend (must be NEXT_PUBLIC_ to reach the browser) -------------------
# Both are deliberately UNSET, and both are inlined at build time.
#
# Unset, the web app uses http://localhost:4000 in a development build (where
# `npm run dev` really does put the API on another port) and same-origin relative
# URLs in a production build (where Nginx or scripts/serve.mjs serves both behind
# one origin). That is the right answer in both cases.
#
# These previously carried localhost values, and that was a live production bug:
# copying this file into a host's variables baked "http://localhost:4000" into
# the bundle, so every visitor's browser tried to reach its own machine.
#
# Set them ONLY when the API is on a genuinely different origin than the web app
# — e.g. two separate services:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL="https://api.example.com"
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCKET_URL="https://api.example.com"

# --- WebRTC ICE ------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma separated STUN urls
STUN_SERVERS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302,stun:stun1.l.google.com:19302
# TURN is REQUIRED for symmetric-NAT / mobile-carrier users. Leave blank to
# run STUN-only (calls will fail for a meaningful % of real-world networks).
TURN_SERVER=
TURN_USERNAME=
TURN_PASSWORD=
# Preferred: coturn "REST API" ephemeral credentials. When TURN_SECRET is set
# the server mints short-lived HMAC credentials per client and TURN_USERNAME /
# TURN_PASSWORD are ignored.  coturn: --use-auth-secret --static-auth-secret=...
TURN_SECRET=
TURN_CREDENTIAL_TTL=86400
TURN_REALM=sonder.local

# --- Calls -----------------------------------------------------------------
# How long an unanswered call rings before the server times it out (ms)
CALL_RING_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
# Grace period a dropped peer has to re-establish before the call is failed (ms)
CALL_RECONNECT_GRACE_MS=30000
